# Commits that changed a great many lines without changing any behaviour.
#
# `git blame` attributes a line to whoever touched it last, so a tree-wide
# mechanical sweep buries the real authorship of everything it touched. Listing
# those commits here makes blame skip straight past them to the change that
# actually wrote the logic.
#
# Enable it once per clone (scripts/dev/setup_hooks.sh does this for you):
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# GitHub honours this file automatically in its blame view.
#
# Only add a commit whose diff is provably behaviour-neutral: a rename, a
# reformat, a header sweep. Never add a commit that also changed logic -- doing
# so hides the logic change from blame, which is exactly the harm this file
# exists to prevent.

